  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1\n\nHigh pressure will give way to an approaching warm front which\nshould push through the region tonight as a low pressure system\ntracks into the Great Lakes. Clouds will be on the increase\ntoday with most spots getting close to 60 for highs. Tonight\nwith the development of a LLJ and warm frontal passage temperatures\nmay not fall much after the early evening hours or even \nslightly rise toward sunrise. Moisture lifted along the warm\nfront looks sufficient enough for a round of showers as well\nheading toward daybreak Friday.  \n\nOverall, the warm sector looks to be in our region slightly\nlonger on Friday now. This should allow for a break in the\nshowers for a time and for high temperatures to make a run at 70\nfor most of the region. Hires models are showing some slight\nCAPE advecting into NEPA and perhaps the Southern Tier of NY as\nwell. Some thunder was added to the forecast ahead of the cold\nfront with the final push of showers moving through the region.\nA few stronger wind gusts may be able to reach the ground with\nany thunderstorm. Rainfall looks to max out around an inch being\naided by terrain in Oneida county with general amounts around\nhalf an inch. \n\nTemperatures drop sharply Friday night but are trending slightly\nwarmer. With the northerly to northwesterly flow lingering\nmoisture from the Great Lakes should be sufficient enough for\nlingering clouds and spotty rain showers and or snow flurries.\nTemperatures look to be stuck in the 30's and 40's Saturday with\na sharp but brief cold shot.  \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2\n\nHigh pressure builds into the region early next week. Winds have\ntrended lighter Saturday night and with clear skies.\nTemperatures still are indicated to fall below freezing for\nmost of the area with the growing season still ongoing in\nLackawanna and Luzerne counties. A fairly tranquil period is \nexpected with high pressure over the region early next week with\nweak southwesterly flow allowing for a warmup.",
